      "title": "Grenades, grief and a deepening political divide: five takeaways from Sonia Gandhi’s new memoir",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/09/this-is-india-sonia-gandhi-memoir-belonging",
      "description": "<p>From the assassinations of her husband and mother-in-law to press stonewalling under Modi, Belonging offers an insider’s account of India’s political evolution</p><p><strong>• </strong><a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/30/sign-up-for-this-is-india-our-free-weekly-newsletter-on-life-in-the-subcontinent\"><strong>Don’t get This is India delivered to your inbox? Sign up here</strong></a></p><p>Few love stories have been as closely entwined with a nation’s history as that of Sonia and Rajiv Gandhi. The former’s much-awaited political memoir, Belonging, offers a strikingly candid account of her journey from a small town in Italy to the highest echelons of power in India – all for love, she says.</p><p>The veteran Congress party leader has been fiercely protective of her personal world. In Belonging, she opens up for the first time about the extraordinary twists and turns of a life that has largely unfolded in full public view. (The Guardian’s Hannah Ellis-Petersen has <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/07/interview-sonia-gandhi-rajiv-gandhi-assassination-memoir-extract\">a rare in-person interview with Gandhi</a>, and you can read <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/07/sonia-gandhi-recalls-killing-indira-gandhi-memoir\">an excerpt from the book here</a>.)</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/09/this-is-india-sonia-gandhi-memoir-belonging\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-10T07:30:31"
    },
    {
      "title": "In Interview, Sonia Gandhi Recalls Love and Loss in India",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/10/world/asia/sonia-gandhi-india.html",
